# Data

This folder is intentionally left empty of raw imagery in the repository (dataset omitted to keep the repo lightweight and respect the original dataset's licensing). Only data-preparation script is included.

## Source

**PV01 / PV03 Solar Panel Segmentation Dataset** (subsets of the *Multi-resolution dataset for photovoltaic panel segmentation from satellite and aerial imagery*)
https://zenodo.org/record/5171712

- **PV01** — used to **train** the model. Split into train/val/test (70/20/10).
- **PV03** — used **only for testing**, to evaluate how well the model generalizes to a different data subset it was never trained on. Not split; the entire PV03 set is held out purely for evaluation. PV03 covers six sub-categories:
  - `PV03_Ground_Cropland`
  - `PV03_Ground_Grassland`
  - `PV03_Ground_SalineAlkali`
  - `PV03_Ground_Shrubwood`
  - `PV03_Ground_WaterSurface`
  - `PV03_Rooftop`

## Setup

1. Download PV01 and PV03 from the Zenodo link above.
2. Place PV01's raw files under `data/PV01/`, and PV03's six category folders under `data/PV03/`.
3. Run the data-splitting script:
   ```bash
   python data_divider.py
   ```
   This script:
   - Splits **PV01 only** into train/val/test (70/20/10, seed=13), stratified per category if applicable, so each split gets a proportional mix
   - Verifies each image has a matching `_label` mask, skipping and warning on any that don't
   - Copies **all of PV03** (no split) into a separate `test_PV03/` folder, kept apart from PV01's own test set
   - Prefixes filenames with their category name to avoid collisions

4. Resulting structure:
   ```
   data/
   ├── PV01/                          <- original downloaded PV01 files
   ├── PV03/                          <- original downloaded PV03 category folders
   ├── train/                         <- PV01 training split
   ├── val/                           <- PV01 validation split
   ├── test/                          <- PV01 held-out test split (in-distribution)
   └── test_PV03/                     <- full PV03 set, test-only (out-of-distribution)
   ```
5. Update `BASE_PATH` in the training script to point to this `data/` folder.

## Why two separate test sets

Reporting metrics on `test/` (PV01) and `test_PV03/` separately gives two distinct signals: performance on data similar to training (in-distribution) versus performance on a related but unseen subset (a basic generalization check). Merging them into one number would obscure this distinction.
